---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import UuidGeneratorTool from '../../components/tools/UuidGeneratorTool.astro';
import { toolBySlug } from '../../data/tools';
import { generateV4, generateV7, extractV7Timestamp, formatUuid, NIL_UUID } from '../../lib/uuid-generate';

const tool = toolBySlug('uuid-generator');

// One example of each, generated by the real engine at build time — random,
// so it changes on every rebuild, which is exactly what a UUID should do.
const exampleV4 = formatUuid(generateV4());
const fixedNow = Date.UTC(2026, 8, 13, 12, 0, 0);
const v7Bytes = generateV7(fixedNow);
const exampleV7 = formatUuid(v7Bytes);
const recoveredTimestamp = new Date(extractV7Timestamp(v7Bytes)).toISOString();

const uppercaseExample = formatUuid(generateV4(), { uppercase: true });
const bracesExample = formatUuid(generateV4(), { braces: true });
const noHyphenExample = formatUuid(generateV4(), { hyphens: false });

const steps = [
  'Choose <strong>Version 4</strong> for a plain random ID, or <strong>Version 7</strong> if the ID also needs to sort roughly by when it was created.',
  'Pick how many to generate at once, from 1 to 100.',
  'Turn on <strong>UPPERCASE</strong>, <strong>Hyphens</strong> or braces to match the format your database or language expects.',
  '<strong>Generate</strong> a fresh batch any time — every click makes new ones. <strong>Copy to clipboard</strong> or <strong>Download .txt</strong> to take them with you.',
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What’s the difference between version 4 and version 7?',
    a: `Version 4 is entirely random — <code>${exampleV4}</code> — so two IDs give no clue which was created first. Version 7 puts a millisecond timestamp in the first part of the UUID and randomizes the rest, so <code>${exampleV7}</code> sorts correctly next to other version 7 UUIDs created before or after it. Reading that timestamp back out of the example above gives <code>${recoveredTimestamp}</code>, exactly the instant it was generated with.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Why would sorting by creation time matter for a database ID?',
    a: 'Databases that use the primary key to physically order rows on disk (most B-tree-indexed tables) suffer from index fragmentation when new keys insert at random positions, which is exactly what version 4 IDs do. Version 7 IDs insert in roughly increasing order, the same way an auto-incrementing integer would, while still being globally unique and unguessable in the random portion.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are these IDs actually unpredictable, or just look random?',
    a: 'Both versions fill their non-timestamp, non-version, non-variant bits from the Web Crypto API’s cryptographically secure random number generator (crypto.getRandomValues) — the same source browsers use for encryption keys — not Math.random(), which is not designed to be unpredictable.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000 a real UUID?',
    a: `It's the "nil" UUID defined by the RFC — a reserved constant meaning "no ID" or "not set," not something this tool would ever generate. If you see <code>${NIL_UUID}</code> somewhere, it usually signals a missing or default value, not an actual generated identifier.`,
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout {tool} {steps} {faq}>
  <UuidGeneratorTool />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>A unique ID with no server round trip</h2>
    <p>
      A UUID (Universally Unique Identifier) is a 128-bit value designed so that two different
      computers, generating IDs independently with no coordination between them, will not collide —
      no database lookup, no central counter, no network call required. That property is what makes
      UUIDs the default choice for database primary keys, request IDs, and anything else that needs
      an identifier before it has anywhere to register one.
    </p>

    <h2>Example</h2>
    <p>A freshly generated version 4 UUID (yours will be different every time — that is the point):</p>
    <pre><code>{exampleV4}</code></pre>
    <p>A version 7 UUID carries its creation time in the first part, recoverable afterward:</p>
    <pre><code>{exampleV7}</code></pre>

    <h2>Options explained</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Version 4 / Version 7</strong> — see the FAQ.</li>
      <li><strong>How many</strong> generates 1 to 100 at once, one per line, so you can paste a
        whole batch of test IDs in one go.</li>
      <li><strong>UPPERCASE</strong> renders hex digits in capitals: <code>{uppercaseExample}</code>.
        <strong>Hyphens</strong> toggles the standard 8-4-4-4-12 grouping; turning it off gives <code>{noHyphenExample}</code>. <strong>Wrap in braces</strong> adds <code>{'{'}</code> and <code>{'}'}</code>
        around each ID: <code>{bracesExample}</code> — the format Microsoft's GUID APIs (and some
        Windows registry tools) expect.</li>
      <li><strong>Generate</strong> makes a new batch with the current settings; the first batch on
        the page is generated automatically so there is one ready immediately.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Edge cases and common errors</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>There is no invalid input</strong> — this tool only generates, so there is nothing
        to reject. The only limit is quantity, capped at 100 per click to keep the page responsive;
        click <strong>Generate</strong> again for more.</li>
      <li><strong>Every batch is independent.</strong> Clicking <strong>Generate</strong> again
        replaces the list rather than appending to it — copy or download a batch before generating a
        new one if you need to keep it.</li>
      <li><strong>Version 7's timestamp is only millisecond-precision</strong> and only as accurate as
        your device's clock; two UUIDs generated in the same millisecond are ordered only by their
        random bits, not guaranteed to reflect which call actually ran first.</li>
      <li><strong>The nil UUID</strong> — see the FAQ.</li>
      <li><strong>Nothing is sent anywhere.</strong> Every ID is generated locally in your browser;
        this page could be disconnected from the network entirely and still work.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Do the same thing in code</h2>
    <pre><code>// JavaScript / browsers and Node 19+ (version 4 only — built in, no library needed)
crypto.randomUUID();

# Python 3.7+ (version 4; version 7 needs the separate uuid6 or uuid-utils package)
import uuid
str(uuid.uuid4())</code></pre>
    <p class="muted">Version 7 is new enough (finalized in RFC 9562, 2024) that few languages'
      standard libraries generate it natively yet — this tool implements the same bit layout by
      hand from the RFC rather than depending on a library.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
